1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:08,422
This photograph shows a room full of young women, bent over factory benches, painting numbers onto watch dials.

2
00:00:08,422 --> 00:00:10,244
Orange, New Jersey, 1922.

3
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:13,159
Nothing in this picture looks dangerous.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:16,994
The paint on her brush glows in the dark.

5
00:00:16,994 --> 00:00:19,359
Her bosses told her it was harmless.

6
00:00:19,359 --> 00:00:22,973
Some of the girls painted their teeth with it, for fun.

7
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:28,161
Within 10 years, her jaw would crumble in a doctor's hands.

8
00:00:28,161 --> 00:00:32,773
Behind almost every photograph you are about to see, somebody died.

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:37,221
The atom has killed with fire and with silence.

10
00:00:37,221 --> 00:00:45,629
It has killed scientists, soldiers, firefighters, scrap dealers, and a 6 year old girl who touched something beautiful.

11
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:51,701
Unlike almost any other killer, it leaves no mark at the moment it strikes.

12
00:00:51,701 --> 00:00:54,004
The victims walk away feeling fine.

13
00:00:54,004 --> 00:00:55,518
The dying starts later.

14
00:00:56,667 --> 00:01:00,165
Some of these deaths were state secrets for 30 years.

15
00:01:00,165 --> 00:01:03,267
Entire poisoned villages were erased from maps.

16
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:07,821
Doctors were forbidden to write the true cause on death certificates.

17
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:11,116
This is the complete story.

18
00:01:11,116 --> 00:01:15,334
Every accident, every cover up, every invisible dose.

19
00:01:15,334 --> 00:01:19,155
Told through the real photographs that survived.

20
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:27,842
By the end, you will understand why physicists speak about radiation the way sailors speak about the sea.

21
00:01:27,842 --> 00:01:28,776
With respect.

22
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:29,782
And with fear.

23
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:31,684
Chapter 1.

24
00:01:31,684 --> 00:01:33,329
The girls who glowed.

25
00:01:34,467 --> 00:01:35,602
New Jersey, 1917.

26
00:01:35,602 --> 00:01:43,949
The United States Radium Corporation hires hundreds of young women to paint watch dials with a miracle product called Undark.

27
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:51,752
The secret ingredient is radium, the glowing element discovered by Marie Curie.

28
00:01:51,752 --> 00:01:54,544
In 1917, radium is a wonder drug.

29
00:01:54,544 --> 00:01:58,860
It is sold in tonics, face creams, even toothpaste.

30
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,161
To paint the tiny numbers, the girls need a fine point on their brushes.

31
00:02:05,161 --> 00:02:08,100
Their supervisors teach them a technique.

32
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:09,247
Lip, dip, paint.

33
00:02:10,367 --> 00:02:14,099
Put the brush between your lips to shape the point.

34
00:02:14,099 --> 00:02:15,636
Dip it in the radium.

35
00:02:15,636 --> 00:02:16,734
Paint the dial.

36
00:02:16,734 --> 00:02:18,563
Then lip the brush again.

37
00:02:18,563 --> 00:02:20,320
Hundreds of times a day.

38
00:02:21,467 --> 00:02:24,661
With every stroke, they swallow a little radium.

39
00:02:24,661 --> 00:02:26,991
And radium has a terrible property.

40
00:02:26,991 --> 00:02:31,184
The body mistakes it for calcium, and builds it into the bones.

41
00:02:32,333 --> 00:02:35,810
Inside the bone, the radium keeps radiating.

42
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:36,916
Day and night.

43
00:02:36,916 --> 00:02:38,180
Year after year.

44
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:41,972
Drilling the skeleton from within, atom by atom.

45
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:49,656
In 1922, a dial painter named Amelia Maggia goes to the dentist with a toothache.

46
00:02:49,656 --> 00:02:51,274
The tooth comes out.

47
00:02:51,274 --> 00:02:52,974
The hole never heals.

48
00:02:54,100 --> 00:03:01,784
Within months, her entire lower jaw has become so brittle that her dentist lifts it out of her mouth with his fingers.

49
00:03:01,784 --> 00:03:03,347
She dies that September.

50
00:03:04,467 --> 00:03:09,062
The company blames syphilis, and writes it on her death certificate.

51
00:03:09,062 --> 00:03:13,793
A lie that smears a dead woman, and buys the corporation 5 more years.

52
00:03:14,933 --> 00:03:17,212
But the girls keep sickening.

53
00:03:17,212 --> 00:03:19,804
Aching hips, loose teeth, anemia.

54
00:03:19,804 --> 00:03:25,774
One by one, the dial painters begin to die, and they begin to compare notes.

55
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:33,794
In 1927, a dying painter named Grace Fryer finds a lawyer willing to take the case.

56
00:03:33,794 --> 00:03:35,705
She has lost her teeth.

57
00:03:35,705 --> 00:03:37,698
Her spine is collapsing.

58
00:03:37,698 --> 00:03:38,446
She sues.

59
00:03:39,567 --> 00:03:44,632
In court, the women are so weak they cannot raise their arms to take the oath.

60
00:03:44,632 --> 00:03:47,359
The newspapers call them the Radium Girls.

61
00:03:47,359 --> 00:03:48,658
The company settles.

62
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:54,698
Dozens of dial painters die of radium poisoning, and their case creates something new.

63
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:58,969
The idea that a company is responsible for what its work does to your body.

64
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:05,027
Decades later, scientists open some of their graves for research.

65
00:04:05,027 --> 00:04:11,243
The bones, after all those years in the ground, still register on the instruments.

66
00:04:11,243 --> 00:04:12,456
Still radiating.

67
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:17,571
The Radium Girls swallowed the atom slowly, over years.

68
00:04:17,571 --> 00:04:20,892
The next men to die would meet it all at once.

69
00:04:20,892 --> 00:04:22,769
In a fraction of a second.

70
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:24,903
Chapter 2.

71
00:04:24,903 --> 00:04:26,408
The demon core.

72
00:04:27,533 --> 00:04:29,665
Los Alamos, New Mexico.

73
00:04:29,665 --> 00:04:30,129
1945.

74
00:04:30,129 --> 00:04:37,080
In a secret laboratory sits a sphere of plutonium the size of a grapefruit.

75
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:38,378
6.2 kilograms.

76
00:04:38,378 --> 00:04:40,046
Warm to the touch.

77
00:04:41,167 --> 00:04:47,454
It was built to be the heart of a third atomic bomb, ready if Japan did not surrender.

78
00:04:47,454 --> 00:04:48,770
Japan surrendered.

79
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:50,963
The core stayed at Los Alamos.

80
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:54,637
So the physicists used it for experiments.

81
00:04:54,637 --> 00:05:01,582
They would nudge the sphere toward criticality, the edge of a chain reaction, and measure how close they could get.

82
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:06,310
The great physicist Richard Feynman had a name for this game.

83
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:09,387
He called it tickling the tail of a sleeping dragon.

84
00:05:10,533 --> 00:05:12,113
There are no interlocks.

85
00:05:12,113 --> 00:05:13,364
No remote handling.

86
00:05:13,364 --> 00:05:20,407
Just steady hands, good judgment, and a wartime culture that treated caution as something you could ration.

87
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:22,733
August 21, 1945.

88
00:05:22,733 --> 00:05:27,982
Physicist Harry Daghlian is working alone at night, against the rules.

89
00:05:27,982 --> 00:05:34,281
He is stacking tungsten carbide bricks around the core, reflecting neutrons back in.

90
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:39,333
One more brick and the assembly will go critical.

91
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:41,580
His instruments tell him so.

92
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:44,069
He moves to pull his hand back.

93
00:05:44,069 --> 00:05:45,353
The brick slips.

94
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:48,771
It lands directly on the core.

95
00:05:48,771 --> 00:05:52,329
There is a flash of blue light, a wave of heat.

96
00:05:52,329 --> 00:05:55,812
In that instant, Harry Daghlian is a dead man.

97
00:05:55,812 --> 00:05:58,386
He just doesn't know the date yet.

98
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:06,673
He calmly disassembles the pile with his bare hand to stop the reaction, then walks himself to the hospital.

99
00:06:06,673 --> 00:06:08,127
He dies 25 days later.

100
00:06:09,267 --> 00:06:12,143
You would think the experiments would stop.

101
00:06:12,143 --> 00:06:13,012
They did not.

102
00:06:13,012 --> 00:06:18,096
9 months later, an even more brilliant physicist stood over the same sphere.

103
00:06:19,233 --> 00:06:21,452
Louis Slotin was the lab's daredevil.

104
00:06:21,452 --> 00:06:29,186
His safety equipment for the experiment was a flathead screwdriver, holding two halves of a beryllium shell apart above the core.

105
00:06:30,333 --> 00:06:32,807
Lower the shell, the reaction builds.

106
00:06:32,807 --> 00:06:34,612
Lift it, the reaction dies.

107
00:06:34,612 --> 00:06:39,091
The screwdriver blade was the only thing keeping the dragon asleep.

108
00:06:39,091 --> 00:06:42,767
Enrico Fermi warned him he would be dead within a year.

109
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:45,090
May 21, 1946.

110
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:50,033
Slotin is demonstrating the technique to 7 colleagues.

111
00:06:50,033 --> 00:06:54,427
At 3:20 in the afternoon, the screwdriver slips.

112
00:06:55,567 --> 00:06:56,717
The shell drops.

113
00:06:56,717 --> 00:06:57,507
Blue flash.

114
00:06:57,507 --> 00:07:00,455
A wave of heat on every face in the room.

115
00:07:00,455 --> 00:07:06,565
Slotin flips the shell off with his bare hand, ending the reaction in under a second.

116
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:09,957
Then he does something extraordinary.

117
00:07:09,957 --> 00:07:16,484
He has everyone mark their exact positions on the floor in chalk, so doctors can calculate each man's dose.

118
00:07:16,484 --> 00:07:19,168
His own body was the shield that saved them.

119
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:25,027
Louis Slotin dies 9 days later, his organs failing one by one.

120
00:07:25,027 --> 00:07:31,432
After that, no human hand at Los Alamos ever touched a criticality experiment again.

121
00:07:31,432 --> 00:07:32,499
Only machines.

122
00:07:33,633 --> 00:07:36,697
The physicists gave the sphere a new name.

123
00:07:36,697 --> 00:07:37,791
The demon core.

124
00:07:37,791 --> 00:07:39,760
It was melted down in 1946.

125
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:44,866
But the thing it was built for had already been tested, in the desert.

126
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:47,012
Chapter 3.

127
00:07:47,012 --> 00:07:49,239
The light before dawn.

128
00:07:50,367 --> 00:07:51,586
July 16, 1945.

129
00:07:51,586 --> 00:07:53,327
5:29 in the morning.

130
00:07:53,327 --> 00:08:00,555
In the New Mexico desert, a light appears that no human being has ever seen before.

131
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:02,862
The Trinity test.

132
00:08:02,862 --> 00:08:05,323
The world's first nuclear explosion.

133
00:08:05,323 --> 00:08:11,339
It turns the night desert brighter than noon, and the flash is seen 280 kilometers away.

134
00:08:12,467 --> 00:08:18,619
Robert Oppenheimer would later say the moment brought a line of Hindu scripture to his mind.

135
00:08:18,619 --> 00:08:21,093
Others simply say he looked relieved.

136
00:08:21,093 --> 00:08:22,498
And very, very quiet.

137
00:08:23,633 --> 00:08:27,858
But here is what the famous photographs do not show.

138
00:08:27,858 --> 00:08:30,296
People lived near that desert.

139
00:08:30,296 --> 00:08:31,515
Ranch families.

140
00:08:31,515 --> 00:08:32,734
Small villages.

141
00:08:32,734 --> 00:08:36,877
Nearly half a million people within 240 kilometers.

142
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,393
Nobody warned them.

143
00:08:39,393 --> 00:08:41,006
Nobody evacuated them.

144
00:08:41,006 --> 00:08:47,091
The test was secret, so the Army told local papers an ammunition dump had exploded.

145
00:08:48,233 --> 00:08:53,720
For days after the blast, a white ash fell from the sky onto the Tularosa Basin.

146
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:55,229
Children played in it.

147
00:08:55,229 --> 00:09:00,510
It settled on gardens, on cattle, on the open rain barrels people drank from.

148
00:09:01,633 --> 00:09:03,091
The ash was fallout.

149
00:09:03,091 --> 00:09:10,959
Irradiated dust from the tower, the ground, and the bomb itself, sucked into the sky and scattered downwind.

150
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:16,803
In the years that followed, families in the basin began keeping lists.

151
00:09:16,803 --> 00:09:19,962
Cancers, in houses that had never known cancer.

152
00:09:19,962 --> 00:09:23,254
Infant deaths in the months right after the test.

153
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:26,536
How many people did Trinity kill?

154
00:09:26,536 --> 00:09:29,060
The honest answer is that nobody knows.

155
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:33,073
No one was counting, because officially, nothing had happened.

156
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:38,355
Rain came days later and washed hot spots into strange patterns.

157
00:09:38,355 --> 00:09:45,041
One ranch family, the Ratliffs, lived under one of the heaviest fallout zones ever measured in America.

158
00:09:46,167 --> 00:09:53,207
The downwinders of New Mexico were not included in the compensation law America later wrote for atomic test victims.

159
00:09:53,207 --> 00:09:56,120
They spent nearly 80 years asking to be counted.

160
00:09:57,267 --> 00:10:00,185
Trinity set the pattern for the atomic age.

161
00:10:00,185 --> 00:10:02,221
The blast gets the photograph.

162
00:10:02,221 --> 00:10:04,189
The fallout gets the silence.

163
00:10:05,333 --> 00:10:13,337
Over the next decades, hundreds of tests would repeat that pattern in Nevada, in Kazakhstan, in the Pacific.

164
00:10:13,337 --> 00:10:15,782
Downwind, the lists kept growing.

165
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:26,382
In Nevada alone, around 100 atmospheric tests would send clouds drifting over towns whose residents were told to watch the flashes as free entertainment.

166
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:32,427
But the deadliest atomic secret of the 1950s was not a bomb test at all.

167
00:10:32,427 --> 00:10:37,217
It was a storage tank, in a Soviet city that did not officially exist.

168
00:10:38,367 --> 00:10:39,195
Chapter 4.

169
00:10:39,195 --> 00:10:41,763
The disaster that didn't exist.

170
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:48,644
In the Ural mountains stands a city you will not find on any Soviet map.

171
00:10:48,644 --> 00:10:49,840
Chelyabinsk 40.

172
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:54,786
Its factory, called Mayak, makes plutonium for Stalin's bombs.

173
00:10:55,933 --> 00:11:00,537
Mayak is built in a desperate hurry, to catch up with America.

174
00:11:00,537 --> 00:11:02,394
Safety is not a priority.

175
00:11:02,394 --> 00:11:07,740
For years, it pumps raw radioactive waste straight into the river Techa.

176
00:11:08,867 --> 00:11:11,526
Villagers downstream drink that river.

177
00:11:11,526 --> 00:11:12,366
Bathe in it.

178
00:11:12,366 --> 00:11:14,186
Water their crops with it.

179
00:11:14,186 --> 00:11:19,786
Some of the most contaminated villages on Earth, and no one tells them anything.

180
00:11:20,933 --> 00:11:26,997
The worst waste, too hot to dump, goes into steel tanks buried in concrete.

181
00:11:26,997 --> 00:11:29,746
The tanks generate their own heat.

182
00:11:29,746 --> 00:11:31,363
They must be cooled.

183
00:11:31,363 --> 00:11:32,009
Forever.

184
00:11:33,133 --> 00:11:37,816
In 1956, the cooling system on tank number 14 begins to fail.

185
00:11:37,816 --> 00:11:39,812
The repairs are postponed.

186
00:11:39,812 --> 00:11:45,646
The tank quietly heats, month after month, like a kettle no one is watching.

187
00:11:46,767 --> 00:11:48,555
September 29, 1957.

188
00:11:48,555 --> 00:11:50,626
4:20 in the afternoon.

189
00:11:50,626 --> 00:11:58,156
The dried-out waste in tank 14 explodes with the force of up to 100 tons of TNT.

190
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:02,722
The 160 ton concrete lid is thrown into the air.

191
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:08,782
A column of radioactive dust rises a kilometer high and drifts northeast on the wind.

192
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:14,380
The plume settles over 20,000 square kilometers of farmland and forest.

193
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:19,617
An invisible scar that scientists would later call the East Ural Radioactive Trace.

194
00:12:20,767 --> 00:12:25,807
In the nearest villages, people see a strange glow in the sky that night.

195
00:12:25,807 --> 00:12:29,950
The newspapers explain it as a rare display of polar lights.

196
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:31,608
Over the southern Urals.

197
00:12:32,733 --> 00:12:34,601
Then the soldiers come.

198
00:12:34,601 --> 00:12:39,798
Around 10,000 people are evacuated, some not for nearly 2 years.

199
00:12:39,798 --> 00:12:42,802
They are ordered to leave everything.

200
00:12:42,802 --> 00:12:44,182
Crops are buried.

201
00:12:44,182 --> 00:12:45,481
Cattle are shot.

202
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:49,086
23 villages are emptied and bulldozed.

203
00:12:49,086 --> 00:12:53,993
Their names are removed from the maps, as if the atom had eaten them whole.

204
00:12:55,133 --> 00:12:56,056
How many died?

205
00:12:56,056 --> 00:12:57,176
Officially, zero.

206
00:12:57,176 --> 00:13:02,647
Independent researchers estimate hundreds of eventual cancer deaths, some say more.

207
00:13:02,647 --> 00:13:06,601
The records that could settle it were sealed, or never kept.

208
00:13:07,733 --> 00:13:11,761
The Soviet Union kept Kyshtym secret for over 30 years.

209
00:13:11,761 --> 00:13:14,617
It only admitted the explosion in 1989.

210
00:13:14,617 --> 00:13:20,402
The third worst nuclear accident in history, and the world simply did not know.

211
00:13:21,533 --> 00:13:23,951
And here is the strangest part.

212
00:13:23,951 --> 00:13:29,645
That very same month, October 1957, the West had its own reactor burning.

213
00:13:29,645 --> 00:13:32,374
And it was keeping its own secrets.

214
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:34,414
Chapter 5.

215
00:13:34,414 --> 00:13:36,426
The fire at Windscale.

216
00:13:37,567 --> 00:13:43,028
On the coast of Cumbria, England, two towers rise over the Windscale plant.

217
00:13:43,028 --> 00:13:49,218
Inside Pile Number 1, tons of graphite and uranium make plutonium for Britain's bomb.

218
00:13:50,367 --> 00:13:52,382
The pile is cooled by open air.

219
00:13:52,382 --> 00:13:57,322
Giant fans blow outside air through the hot core, and out through a chimney.

220
00:13:57,322 --> 00:14:01,678
A design one physicist compared to a chimney with a furnace inside.

221
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:10,716
Graphite bombarded by neutrons stores hidden energy, and it must be released with careful controlled heating.

222
00:14:10,716 --> 00:14:15,146
On October 8, 1957, the operators run that heating procedure.

223
00:14:15,146 --> 00:14:16,671
Something goes wrong.

224
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:20,461
Deep in the core, fuel begins to burn.

225
00:14:20,461 --> 00:14:22,422
For 2 days, nobody realizes.

226
00:14:22,422 --> 00:14:27,674
The instruments were never designed to see a fire in that part of the pile.

227
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:29,527
October 10.

228
00:14:29,527 --> 00:14:34,350
A worker checks the core through an inspection hole and sees it directly.

229
00:14:34,350 --> 00:14:36,332
Fuel channels glowing red hot.

230
00:14:36,332 --> 00:14:38,909
Britain's plutonium factory is on fire.

231
00:14:40,033 --> 00:14:49,363
Deputy manager Tom Tuohy climbs to the top of the reactor again and again, and looks straight down into the burning core to judge the flames.

232
00:14:49,363 --> 00:14:51,017
Practically no shielding.

233
00:14:51,017 --> 00:14:52,075
Somebody had to.

234
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:59,850
Below him, volunteers with scaffolding poles push burning fuel cartridges out of the channels by brute force.

235
00:14:59,850 --> 00:15:02,291
Some rods glow so hot they jam in place.

236
00:15:03,433 --> 00:15:05,333
They try carbon dioxide.

237
00:15:05,333 --> 00:15:06,678
The fire eats it.

238
00:15:06,678 --> 00:15:14,117
Eleven tons of uranium are burning at over 1,000 degrees, and the temperature is still rising.

239
00:15:15,267 --> 00:15:19,404
Tuohy is left with one option, and it terrifies everyone.

240
00:15:19,404 --> 00:15:19,839
Water.

241
00:15:19,839 --> 00:15:23,106
Water on burning metal can generate hydrogen.

242
00:15:23,106 --> 00:15:26,735
The pile could explode and tear the building open.

243
00:15:27,867 --> 00:15:35,677
On the morning of October 11, they turn on the hoses and shut off the cooling fans, starving the fire of air.

244
00:15:35,677 --> 00:15:37,253
Slowly, the glow dies.

245
00:15:37,253 --> 00:15:40,693
Windscale has come within a step of catastrophe.

246
00:15:41,833 --> 00:15:46,537
But for 2 days, the chimney has been breathing smoke across England.

247
00:15:46,537 --> 00:15:52,831
Radioactive iodine, drifting over farms, settling on grass, passing through cows into milk.

248
00:15:53,967 --> 00:16:01,683
The government bans milk from 500 square kilometers of countryside and quietly pours 2 million liters into the sea.

249
00:16:01,683 --> 00:16:04,233
The public is told there is no danger.

250
00:16:05,367 --> 00:16:07,688
No one died at Windscale that week.

251
00:16:07,688 --> 00:16:10,076
That is what makes it so unsettling.

252
00:16:10,076 --> 00:16:11,801
The dying was statistical.

253
00:16:11,801 --> 00:16:18,036
Later studies estimate the release eventually caused on the order of 100 to 240 cancer deaths.

254
00:16:19,167 --> 00:16:21,263
You cannot point to the victims.

255
00:16:21,263 --> 00:16:25,979
They are scattered through decades of ordinary death records, invisible.

256
00:16:25,979 --> 00:16:28,075
The atom's favorite way to kill.

257
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:35,630
The full report on the fire was suppressed for 30 years, by the Prime Minister personally.

258
00:16:35,630 --> 00:16:41,131
And 4 years later, in an American desert, the atom stopped being statistical.

259
00:16:41,131 --> 00:16:42,131
It got direct.

260
00:16:43,267 --> 00:16:44,036
Chapter 6.

261
00:16:44,036 --> 00:16:46,114
Nine feet from the ceiling.

262
00:16:47,233 --> 00:16:49,067
Idaho, January 3, 1961.

263
00:16:49,067 --> 00:16:56,559
In the frozen desert outside Idaho Falls stands a small experimental Army reactor called SL-1.

264
00:16:56,559 --> 00:17:01,182
Tonight, 3 young men are restarting it after the holidays.

265
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:03,577
Jack Byrnes, 27.

266
00:17:03,577 --> 00:17:04,934
Richard Legg, 26.

267
00:17:04,934 --> 00:17:06,610
Richard McKinley, 22.

268
00:17:06,610 --> 00:17:15,309
Army and Navy technicians, trained to run a reactor designed to power radar stations at the top of the world.

269
00:17:16,433 --> 00:17:19,628
SL-1 has a design flaw that reads like a dare.

270
00:17:19,628 --> 00:17:28,240
It is so small that pulling out just one control rod, the central one, can take the reactor from dead cold to supercritical.

271
00:17:29,367 --> 00:17:34,024
The procedure that night requires lifting that central rod by hand.

272
00:17:34,024 --> 00:17:38,196
10 centimeters, no more, to reconnect it to its drive motor.

273
00:17:39,333 --> 00:17:44,853
At 9:01 pm, the rod comes up roughly 50 centimeters in under a second.

274
00:17:44,853 --> 00:17:47,849
To this day, no one knows exactly why.

275
00:17:48,967 --> 00:17:54,823
The reactor goes from essentially zero to nearly 20,000 megawatts in about 4 milliseconds.

276
00:17:54,823 --> 00:17:59,573
The water above the core flashes to steam and slams upward like a piston.

277
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:07,471
The entire 13 ton reactor vessel jumps 3 meters into the air, shearing its pipes, before crashing back down.

278
00:18:07,471 --> 00:18:10,731
Everyone in the building is killed almost instantly.

279
00:18:11,867 --> 00:18:15,685
When the alarm sounds, firefighters arrive to a silent building.

280
00:18:15,685 --> 00:18:21,114
Their radiation meters climb to the top of the scale at the door, and keep trying to climb.

281
00:18:22,233 --> 00:18:26,926
Inside, rescuers working in shifts of seconds find 2 of the men.

282
00:18:26,926 --> 00:18:28,539
Then someone looks up.

283
00:18:28,539 --> 00:18:30,592
The third man is above them.

284
00:18:31,733 --> 00:18:34,850
Richard Legg had been standing on top of the reactor.

285
00:18:34,850 --> 00:18:40,672
A shield plug shot from the vessel like a cannon and pinned him to the ceiling of the reactor room.

286
00:18:40,672 --> 00:18:42,260
He stayed there for 6 days.

287
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:51,524
Recovering him took a team with a crane and a net, rehearsed on a mannequin, with each man allowed only 65 seconds of exposure inside.

288
00:18:52,667 --> 00:18:58,929
The bodies themselves were so radioactive they were buried in lead-lined coffins under concrete.

289
00:18:58,929 --> 00:19:04,474
The most exposed body parts were removed and buried separately, as radioactive waste.

290
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:11,167
SL-1 remains the only reactor accident in American history to cause immediate deaths.

291
00:19:11,167 --> 00:19:14,769
3 men, killed by a machine built to keep soldiers warm.

292
00:19:15,900 --> 00:19:20,613
Rumors of sabotage and a love triangle followed the case for decades.

293
00:19:20,613 --> 00:19:26,009
The official cause remains a rod pulled too far, too fast, for reasons unknown.

294
00:19:27,133 --> 00:19:34,282
That same year, on the far side of the world, another crew of young men was learning what a broken reactor demands.

295
00:19:34,282 --> 00:19:35,649
Theirs was underwater.

296
00:19:36,767 --> 00:19:37,685
Chapter 7.

297
00:19:37,685 --> 00:19:39,614
Into the compartment.

298
00:19:40,733 --> 00:19:41,771
July 4, 1961.

299
00:19:41,771 --> 00:19:43,287
The North Atlantic.

300
00:19:43,287 --> 00:19:52,462
K-19, the Soviet Union's first ballistic missile submarine, is running exercises when a gauge needle drops to zero.

301
00:19:53,600 --> 00:19:57,205
Pressure lost in the reactor's primary cooling circuit.

302
00:19:57,205 --> 00:19:58,319
A pipe has burst.

303
00:19:58,319 --> 00:20:04,284
Coolant is draining away, and the reactor core has begun to heat itself toward destruction.

304
00:20:05,433 --> 00:20:07,799
The boat was cursed from birth.

305
00:20:07,799 --> 00:20:09,783
Workers died building her.

306
00:20:09,783 --> 00:20:13,674
The champagne bottle failed to break at her launch.

307
00:20:13,674 --> 00:20:16,039
Her crews called her Hiroshima.

308
00:20:17,167 --> 00:20:19,831
There is no backup cooling system.

309
00:20:19,831 --> 00:20:22,496
The designers never installed one.

310
00:20:22,496 --> 00:20:31,038
If the core melts, the Soviet Navy loses a submarine, 139 men, and possibly much more, off the coast of NATO.

311
00:20:32,167 --> 00:20:34,379
The engineers improvise a solution.

312
00:20:34,379 --> 00:20:36,844
Weld a new water line onto the reactor.

313
00:20:36,844 --> 00:20:40,257
But that means men going into the reactor compartment.

314
00:20:40,257 --> 00:20:43,165
Everyone on board understands what that means.

315
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:47,715
Lieutenant Boris Korchilov leads the first team in.

316
00:20:47,715 --> 00:20:53,809
Their protection is chemical suits and gas masks, gear designed for other dangers entirely.

317
00:20:53,809 --> 00:20:56,421
Against gamma radiation, it is costume.

318
00:20:57,567 --> 00:21:04,960
They work in the steam and heat, welding, while the compartment fills with radiation beyond anything their instruments can honestly measure.

319
00:21:06,100 --> 00:21:08,405
The men come out changed.

320
00:21:08,405 --> 00:21:11,264
Faces swelling, skin reddening.

321
00:21:11,264 --> 00:21:14,123
Within hours some cannot speak.

322
00:21:14,123 --> 00:21:16,613
The jury-rigged line works.

323
00:21:16,613 --> 00:21:18,273
The core is saved.

324
00:21:18,273 --> 00:21:21,408
The price is being paid in bodies.

325
00:21:22,533 --> 00:21:25,744
8 men, including Korchilov, die within weeks.

326
00:21:25,744 --> 00:21:29,597
Around 14 more of the crew die in the following years.

327
00:21:29,597 --> 00:21:32,094
The survivors are sworn to secrecy.

328
00:21:33,233 --> 00:21:38,771
For decades, Soviet doctors are forbidden to write radiation sickness on such records.

329
00:21:38,771 --> 00:21:42,313
The files say things like asthenic vegetative syndrome.

330
00:21:42,313 --> 00:21:45,275
A diagnosis invented to hide a cause of death.

331
00:21:46,400 --> 00:21:50,178
In 1968, it happens again, on another submarine.

332
00:21:50,178 --> 00:21:56,475
K-27, with an experimental liquid metal reactor, suffers a power failure at sea.

333
00:21:56,475 --> 00:21:58,521
Radiation floods the boat.

334
00:21:59,667 --> 00:22:03,225
Again, sailors go into the reactor compartment.

335
00:22:03,225 --> 00:22:05,345
Again, they come out to die.

336
00:22:05,345 --> 00:22:08,071
9 crewmen are killed by their doses.

337
00:22:08,071 --> 00:22:10,116
The youngest are barely 20.

338
00:22:11,233 --> 00:22:17,721
The Soviet Navy could not repair K-27, so in 1982 it solved the problem another way.

339
00:22:17,721 --> 00:22:24,054
It scuttled the submarine, reactor and all, in the shallow waters of the Kara Sea.

340
00:22:24,054 --> 00:22:25,444
It is still there.

341
00:22:26,567 --> 00:22:29,296
The submariners met the atom in disasters.

342
00:22:29,296 --> 00:22:36,833
But through those same decades, it was quietly killing men one at a time, in ordinary rooms, on ordinary afternoons.

343
00:22:37,967 --> 00:22:39,001
Chapter 8.

344
00:22:39,001 --> 00:22:40,553
The blue flash.

345
00:22:41,700 --> 00:22:45,923
There is a category of nuclear accident with its own grim signature.

346
00:22:45,923 --> 00:22:47,475
The criticality accident.

347
00:22:47,475 --> 00:22:52,071
Too much fissile material, too close together, for a fraction of a second.

348
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:56,436
The survivors' accounts agree on the details.

349
00:22:56,436 --> 00:22:58,019
A flash of blue light.

350
00:22:58,019 --> 00:22:59,961
A wave of heat on the skin.

351
00:22:59,961 --> 00:23:04,276
Sometimes a metallic taste, flooding the mouth from nowhere.

352
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:08,706
The blue is the air itself, ionized by radiation.

353
00:23:08,706 --> 00:23:15,117
If you are close enough to see it clearly, you have very likely already received a lethal dose.

354
00:23:16,267 --> 00:23:20,008
There have been around 60 criticality accidents worldwide.

355
00:23:20,008 --> 00:23:23,040
They happen in the most mundane way imaginable.

356
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:25,749
Usually, someone is just pouring a liquid.

357
00:23:26,867 --> 00:23:28,104
Los Alamos, 1958.

358
00:23:28,104 --> 00:23:35,452
Chemist Cecil Kelley switches on a mixing tank that, unknown to him, contains far too much plutonium.

359
00:23:35,452 --> 00:23:37,708
The blue flash lights the room.

360
00:23:38,833 --> 00:23:43,581
Kelley receives one of the largest radiation doses ever recorded.

361
00:23:43,581 --> 00:23:45,262
He dies 35 hours later.

362
00:23:45,262 --> 00:23:51,032
His case becomes a foundational study in what massive doses do to a human body.

363
00:23:52,167 --> 00:23:54,793
Vinca, Yugoslavia, that same year.

364
00:23:54,793 --> 00:24:01,436
6 young researchers are dosed when an unshielded research reactor runs out of control.

365
00:24:01,436 --> 00:24:02,980
Nobody sees a flash.

366
00:24:02,980 --> 00:24:04,680
They only smell ozone.

367
00:24:05,800 --> 00:24:10,757
They are flown to Paris, where doctors attempt something nearly unprecedented.

368
00:24:10,757 --> 00:24:17,111
Bone marrow transplants, from volunteer French donors, to rebuild the blood the radiation destroyed.

369
00:24:18,233 --> 00:24:19,562
5 of the 6 survive.

370
00:24:19,562 --> 00:24:21,519
One, the youngest, does not.

371
00:24:21,519 --> 00:24:30,746
The Vinca accident helps invent modern bone marrow transplantation, a treatment that has since saved hundreds of thousands of lives.

372
00:24:31,867 --> 00:24:34,763
Wood River Junction, Rhode Island, 1964.

373
00:24:34,763 --> 00:24:43,596
Robert Peabody, a 37 year old plant worker, pours what he believes is a low-grade solution into a tank of stirring liquid.

374
00:24:44,733 --> 00:24:47,707
The bottle actually holds concentrated uranium.

375
00:24:47,707 --> 00:24:50,175
The tank flashes critical in his hands.

376
00:24:50,175 --> 00:24:56,122
He is thrown backward, drenched, and receives roughly twice the dose that killed Cecil Kelley.

377
00:24:57,267 --> 00:24:59,736
Robert Peabody dies 49 hours later.

378
00:24:59,736 --> 00:25:07,638
A husband, a father of 9, killed by 2 identical-looking bottles and a labeling system that let them be confused.

379
00:25:08,767 --> 00:25:11,757
That is the pattern of the blue flash.

380
00:25:11,757 --> 00:25:14,196
Not villains, not recklessness.

381
00:25:14,196 --> 00:25:15,455
A slipped brick.

382
00:25:15,455 --> 00:25:17,029
A mislabeled bottle.

383
00:25:17,029 --> 00:25:21,515
Geometry and gravity doing the rest, faster than thought.

384
00:25:22,633 --> 00:25:26,013
Every one of these accidents killed men in ones and twos.

385
00:25:26,013 --> 00:25:28,741
The atom's worst night would kill differently.

386
00:25:28,741 --> 00:25:30,757
It would take a whole map with it.

387
00:25:31,900 --> 00:25:32,875
Chapter 9.

388
00:25:32,875 --> 00:25:34,826
1:23 in the morning.

389
00:25:35,967 --> 00:25:37,155
April 26, 1986.

390
00:25:37,155 --> 00:25:38,343
Soviet Ukraine.

391
00:25:38,343 --> 00:25:48,088
At the Chernobyl nuclear power plant, the night shift of reactor number 4 is running a safety test that is 3 years overdue.

392
00:25:49,233 --> 00:25:55,655
The test has been delayed all day, so it lands on the least experienced shift, after midnight.

393
00:25:55,655 --> 00:26:00,231
The reactor has been held in an unstable, poisoned state for hours.

394
00:26:01,367 --> 00:26:05,579
The RBMK reactor has a flaw its operators were never told about.

395
00:26:05,579 --> 00:26:08,015
At low power, it becomes treacherous.

396
00:26:08,015 --> 00:26:13,018
And its emergency shutdown button has a defect that reads like a cruel joke.

397
00:26:14,167 --> 00:26:19,846
For the first fraction of a second, pressing the shutdown button can make the reaction speed up.

398
00:26:19,846 --> 00:26:23,336
On this night, in this state, that is exactly what it does.

399
00:26:24,467 --> 00:26:30,530
At 1:23 and 40 seconds, shift supervisor Alexander Akimov orders the shutdown.

400
00:26:30,530 --> 00:26:33,717
Power surges to perhaps 100 times normal.

401
00:26:33,717 --> 00:26:35,116
The fuel shatters.

402
00:26:35,116 --> 00:26:37,215
The water flashes to steam.

403
00:26:38,333 --> 00:26:44,245
Two explosions tear the reactor apart and throw its 1,000 ton lid aside like a coin.

404
00:26:44,245 --> 00:26:50,297
The core, the burning graphite, the fuel, all of it is suddenly open to the night sky.

405
00:26:51,433 --> 00:26:54,118
One man dies in the blast itself.

406
00:26:54,118 --> 00:26:57,943
Valery Khodemchuk, a circulation pump operator.

407
00:26:57,943 --> 00:27:00,221
His body is never recovered.

408
00:27:00,221 --> 00:27:03,475
The sarcophagus is his tomb to this day.

409
00:27:04,600 --> 00:27:07,839
In the control room, the instruments make no sense.

410
00:27:07,839 --> 00:27:12,540
Akimov and young engineer Leonid Toptunov cannot believe the core is gone.

411
00:27:12,540 --> 00:27:17,113
They spend the night pumping water into a reactor that no longer exists.

412
00:27:18,233 --> 00:27:23,994
Both will die of radiation sickness within weeks, still insisting they did everything right.

413
00:27:23,994 --> 00:27:26,435
The terrible truth is, they mostly had.

414
00:27:27,567 --> 00:27:33,556
The first firefighters arrive within minutes, led by 23 year old Lieutenant Vladimir Pravik.

415
00:27:33,556 --> 00:27:37,787
They climb onto the roof of the turbine hall to fight the flames.

416
00:27:37,787 --> 00:27:40,001
Nobody tells them what is burning.

417
00:27:41,133 --> 00:27:43,710
Their dosimeters are not built for this.

418
00:27:43,710 --> 00:27:48,025
The scale of the plant's best instruments ends far below the truth.

419
00:27:48,025 --> 00:27:50,537
The men on the roof are fighting blind.

420
00:27:51,667 --> 00:27:55,271
Chunks of the reactor core lie scattered around them.

421
00:27:55,271 --> 00:27:59,283
Graphite from inside the reactor, on the ground, radiating.

422
00:27:59,283 --> 00:28:02,003
Some firefighters kick the blocks aside.

423
00:28:02,003 --> 00:28:04,180
One reportedly picks a piece up.

424
00:28:05,300 --> 00:28:09,474
Among them is Vasily Ignatenko, 25 years old, married 3 years.

425
00:28:09,474 --> 00:28:16,611
His wife Lyudmilla watches from their apartment as the men work under the strange glowing column of light.

426
00:28:17,733 --> 00:28:22,234
By morning, the firefighters are vomiting, their faces darkening.

427
00:28:22,234 --> 00:28:25,489
They are flown to a special hospital in Moscow.

428
00:28:25,489 --> 00:28:30,820
Lyudmilla follows Vasily, hides her pregnancy, and refuses to leave his side.

429
00:28:31,967 --> 00:28:37,816
The nurses warn her that her husband is no longer a person she can safely touch.

430
00:28:37,816 --> 00:28:41,033
He is, in their words, a radioactive object.

431
00:28:41,033 --> 00:28:44,323
She stays anyway, for 14 days, until the end.

432
00:28:45,467 --> 00:28:51,895
28 plant workers and firefighters die of acute radiation sickness in the weeks after the explosion.

433
00:28:51,895 --> 00:28:56,700
They are buried in Moscow, in soldered zinc coffins, under concrete slabs.

434
00:28:57,833 --> 00:29:04,684
36 hours after the blast, the city of Pripyat, 50,000 people, is finally evacuated.

435
00:29:04,684 --> 00:29:07,160
They are told it is temporary.

436
00:29:07,160 --> 00:29:08,728
Three days at most.

437
00:29:08,728 --> 00:29:11,782
Their plates are still on the tables.

438
00:29:12,900 --> 00:29:14,154
The fire is out.

439
00:29:14,154 --> 00:29:15,564
The city is empty.

440
00:29:15,564 --> 00:29:22,304
And the real war, the one fought by half a million ordinary people, is only beginning.

441
00:29:23,433 --> 00:29:24,487
Chapter 10.

442
00:29:24,487 --> 00:29:26,019
The liquidators.

443
00:29:27,167 --> 00:29:31,686
The Soviet Union mobilizes for Chernobyl like a world war.

444
00:29:31,686 --> 00:29:37,997
Around 600,000 people, soldiers, miners, pilots, drivers, are sent into the zone.

445
00:29:37,997 --> 00:29:40,724
History calls them the liquidators.

446
00:29:41,867 --> 00:29:45,283
First problem: the burning core is open to the sky.

447
00:29:45,283 --> 00:29:52,786
Helicopter crews fly directly over the glowing crater, dropping sand, lead, and boron into the reactor's throat.

448
00:29:53,933 --> 00:29:59,012
Pilots take doses in minutes that workers should not take in years.

449
00:29:59,012 --> 00:30:04,848
In October 1986, one helicopter's blades clip a crane cable near the reactor.

450
00:30:04,848 --> 00:30:06,667
It falls out of the sky.

451
00:30:06,667 --> 00:30:07,804
All 4 crew die.

452
00:30:08,933 --> 00:30:18,076
Second problem: melted fuel may burn down toward water beneath the reactor, risking a second, far worse steam explosion.

453
00:30:18,076 --> 00:30:20,210
The water has to be drained.

454
00:30:20,210 --> 00:30:20,819
By hand.

455
00:30:21,967 --> 00:30:29,972
Three men, Ananenko, Bezpalov, and Baranov, wade through flooded, radioactive corridors in the dark to find the valves.

456
00:30:29,972 --> 00:30:34,009
The world would later tell their story as a suicide mission.

457
00:30:35,133 --> 00:30:36,919
They found the valves.

458
00:30:36,919 --> 00:30:38,299
They opened them.

459
00:30:38,299 --> 00:30:44,550
And, contrary to the legend that they died within days, all 3 came out alive.

460
00:30:44,550 --> 00:30:46,092
The dread was real.

461
00:30:46,092 --> 00:30:47,959
The martyrdom was myth.

462
00:30:49,100 --> 00:30:50,717
Third problem: the roof.

463
00:30:50,717 --> 00:30:57,119
Chunks of reactor core lie on the rooftops next to the crater, too radioactive for any machine.

464
00:30:57,119 --> 00:31:00,489
The robots sent up fail, their circuits scrambled.

465
00:31:01,633 --> 00:31:06,403
So the Soviet Union sends what the officers grimly call biorobots.

466
00:31:06,403 --> 00:31:12,474
Men, in lead aprons, running onto the roof to shovel reactor graphite over the edge.

467
00:31:13,600 --> 00:31:18,533
Each man is allowed one run of roughly 40 to 90 seconds.

468
00:31:18,533 --> 00:31:22,673
A siren, a sprint, a few shovel loads, and out.

469
00:31:22,673 --> 00:31:24,699
That is his entire war.

470
00:31:24,699 --> 00:31:28,751
Roughly 3,000 men take their turn on the roof.

471
00:31:29,900 --> 00:31:34,308
Miners tunnel under the reactor in brutal heat to install cooling.

472
00:31:34,308 --> 00:31:36,913
Reservists shovel contaminated topsoil.

473
00:31:36,913 --> 00:31:42,256
Hunters shoot the abandoned pets of Pripyat so they cannot spread contamination.

474
00:31:43,400 --> 00:31:47,596
Many liquidators later received medals, and pensions that inflation ate.

475
00:31:47,596 --> 00:31:54,006
Their registered death and illness counts remain among the most bitterly disputed numbers of the 20th century.

476
00:31:55,133 --> 00:32:02,870
By November, the liquidators have buried reactor 4 inside a vast grey shell of steel and concrete.

477
00:32:02,870 --> 00:32:04,133
The sarcophagus.

478
00:32:04,133 --> 00:32:09,422
Built in 206 days, by men working in doses no meter fully recorded.

479
00:32:10,567 --> 00:32:13,010
So how many people did Chernobyl kill?

480
00:32:13,010 --> 00:32:17,254
Choose your source, and the answer changes by orders of magnitude.

481
00:32:18,400 --> 00:32:24,539
The confirmed count is around 31 immediate deaths, plus dozens more among the acute survivors.

482
00:32:24,539 --> 00:32:31,069
The World Health Organization projected roughly 4,000 eventual cancer deaths among the most exposed.

483
00:32:32,200 --> 00:32:36,577
Other studies argue for tens of thousands across Europe over decades.

484
00:32:36,577 --> 00:32:43,746
The honest truth is that the full toll is genuinely unknowable, hidden inside ordinary cancer statistics forever.

485
00:32:44,867 --> 00:32:47,911
Chernobyl was the atom at its most enormous.

486
00:32:47,911 --> 00:32:51,093
The next disaster on our list is the opposite.

487
00:32:51,093 --> 00:32:54,898
It fit inside a steel capsule the size of a coffee tin.

488
00:32:56,033 --> 00:32:56,982
Chapter 11.

489
00:32:56,982 --> 00:32:58,619
The glowing powder.

490
00:32:59,767 --> 00:33:01,103
Goiania, Brazil.

491
00:33:01,103 --> 00:33:02,356
September 1987.

492
00:33:02,356 --> 00:33:08,621
In the ruins of an abandoned cancer clinic, a machine has been left behind.

493
00:33:08,621 --> 00:33:14,134
Inside it, sealed in steel and lead, sits a capsule of cesium-137.

494
00:33:15,267 --> 00:33:21,607
For 2 years, the source sits in the derelict building while owners and courts argue about the property.

495
00:33:21,607 --> 00:33:25,794
A city of a million people, and a radiotherapy source with no guard.

496
00:33:26,933 --> 00:33:34,575
On September 13, two scavengers looking for scrap metal wheel the machine's rotating head home in a wheelbarrow.

497
00:33:34,575 --> 00:33:36,895
To them it is just steel and lead.

498
00:33:36,895 --> 00:33:37,304
Money.

499
00:33:38,433 --> 00:33:47,027
They spend days breaking it open, both growing strangely ill, vomiting, one's hand beginning to burn and swell where he works on the capsule.

500
00:33:48,167 --> 00:33:53,001
Eventually they puncture the capsule window, and find something wonderful inside.

501
00:33:53,001 --> 00:33:58,433
A powder that glows deep blue in the dark, like carnival glitter, like something enchanted.

502
00:33:59,567 --> 00:34:03,598
The remains are sold to a scrapyard owner, Devair Ferreira.

503
00:34:03,598 --> 00:34:04,759
He is mesmerized.

504
00:34:04,759 --> 00:34:11,113
He brings the glowing material into his house and shows it off to family, friends, neighbors.

505
00:34:12,233 --> 00:34:14,000
People take pinches of it.

506
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:17,126
They rub it on their skin to shine at parties.

507
00:34:17,126 --> 00:34:23,309
Fragments travel across the city in pockets, on bus seats, on hands that then prepare food.

508
00:34:24,433 --> 00:34:30,737
Devair's 6 year old niece, Leide das Neves, plays with the powder on the floor while eating a sandwich.

509
00:34:30,737 --> 00:34:32,757
Some of the glow is on her hands.

510
00:34:32,757 --> 00:34:34,960
Some of it goes down with the bread.

511
00:34:36,100 --> 00:34:39,606
Within days, an invisible epidemic spreads.

512
00:34:39,606 --> 00:34:42,378
Vomiting, burns, hair falling out.

513
00:34:42,378 --> 00:34:47,514
Doctors diagnose everything from allergies to tropical disease.

514
00:34:47,514 --> 00:34:49,715
Nobody thinks of radiation.

515
00:34:49,715 --> 00:34:50,938
Why would they?

516
00:34:52,067 --> 00:34:56,905
It is Devair's wife, Gabriela, already gravely ill, who ends it.

517
00:34:56,905 --> 00:35:06,356
She hauls the remains of the source in a plastic bag, by bus, to a public health clinic, and says: this is killing my family.

518
00:35:07,500 --> 00:35:16,591
A visiting physicist with a borrowed detector traces the contamination and raises the alarm, reportedly stopping first responders from dumping the bag in a river.

519
00:35:17,733 --> 00:35:22,726
112,000 people line up at the Olympic stadium to be scanned.

520
00:35:22,726 --> 00:35:24,474
249 are contaminated.

521
00:35:24,474 --> 00:35:26,305
Houses are demolished.

522
00:35:26,305 --> 00:35:27,969
Topsoil is stripped.

523
00:35:27,969 --> 00:35:32,962
The cleanup fills thousands of drums with radioactive waste.

524
00:35:34,100 --> 00:35:37,948
For years afterward, some of Goiania's people were shunned.

525
00:35:37,948 --> 00:35:44,209
Taxis refused them, hotels turned them away, as if the invisible thing could be caught like flu.

526
00:35:45,333 --> 00:35:51,015
4 people die within weeks, including Gabriela, who saved the city, and little Leide.

527
00:35:51,015 --> 00:35:57,846
The girl is buried in a lead coffin, under concrete, while frightened crowds protest at the cemetery.

528
00:35:58,967 --> 00:36:03,853
Goiania remains the textbook warning about what one lost source can do to a city.

529
00:36:03,853 --> 00:36:09,886
12 years later, another routine workplace would produce the atomic age's most haunting medical case.

530
00:36:11,033 --> 00:36:12,727
Chapter 12.

531
00:36:12,727 --> 00:36:13,959
83 days.

532
00:36:15,100 --> 00:36:16,471
Tokaimura, Japan.

533
00:36:16,471 --> 00:36:18,004
September 30, 1999.

534
00:36:18,004 --> 00:36:26,313
At a small fuel processing plant, 3 workers are preparing uranium solution for an experimental reactor.

535
00:36:26,313 --> 00:36:28,814
They are badly behind schedule.

536
00:36:29,933 --> 00:36:34,451
The approved procedure is slow, so the plant has developed shortcuts.

537
00:36:34,451 --> 00:36:39,886
Instead of controlled equipment, the workers mix uranium in steel buckets, by hand.

538
00:36:41,033 --> 00:36:48,199
Hisashi Ouchi, 35, holds a funnel over a precipitation tank while a colleague pours in bucket after bucket.

539
00:36:48,199 --> 00:36:53,154
The tank was never meant to hold this much enriched uranium in this shape.

540
00:36:54,300 --> 00:36:58,361
At the seventh bucket, the solution reaches critical mass.

541
00:36:58,361 --> 00:36:59,901
There is a blue flash.

542
00:36:59,901 --> 00:37:06,342
Ouchi is standing directly over the tank, closer than almost any accident victim in history.

543
00:37:07,467 --> 00:37:10,366
He receives an estimated 17 sieverts.

544
00:37:10,366 --> 00:37:13,344
The dose considered fatal is around 8.

545
00:37:13,344 --> 00:37:17,263
His colleague Masato Shinohara receives around 10.

546
00:37:17,263 --> 00:37:20,476
Their supervisor, further away, around 3.

547
00:37:21,600 --> 00:37:24,297
At first, Ouchi seems almost fine.

548
00:37:24,297 --> 00:37:30,087
He is conscious, talking, with little more than reddened skin and nausea.

549
00:37:30,087 --> 00:37:32,783
This is the atom's cruelest trick.

550
00:37:32,783 --> 00:37:34,687
The walking ghost phase.

551
00:37:35,833 --> 00:37:40,458
The damage is already total, written into him at the level of DNA.

552
00:37:40,458 --> 00:37:45,012
The radiation has destroyed his body's ability to make new cells.

553
00:37:45,012 --> 00:37:47,954
Everything that must renew itself, cannot.

554
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:53,452
Japan's finest doctors fight for him with everything medicine has.

555
00:37:53,452 --> 00:37:58,464
Transfusions, skin grafts, pioneering stem cell transplants from his sister.

556
00:37:58,464 --> 00:38:01,299
For a moment, the new cells even take hold.

557
00:38:02,433 --> 00:38:06,981
Then the radiation still lodged in his body damages the transplanted cells too.

558
00:38:06,981 --> 00:38:11,759
The doctors are trying to rebuild a house while the fire is still inside the walls.

559
00:38:12,900 --> 00:38:16,104
His heart stops 3 times in one hour on day 59.

560
00:38:16,104 --> 00:38:25,021
Each time, he is revived, at his family's request, while the medical team quietly begins to question what they are fighting for.

561
00:38:26,167 --> 00:38:29,709
Hisashi Ouchi dies on December 21, 1999.

562
00:38:29,709 --> 00:38:31,834
He had survived 83 days.

563
00:38:31,834 --> 00:38:42,284
His case is studied worldwide, and it hardened a consensus: beyond a certain dose, medicine can prolong, but not save.

564
00:38:43,433 --> 00:38:47,491
Masato Shinohara fights for 7 months before he too dies.

565
00:38:47,491 --> 00:38:49,230
The supervisor survives.

566
00:38:49,230 --> 00:38:56,259
The company, JCO, is found to have rewritten safety procedures for speed, with regulators asleep.

567
00:38:57,400 --> 00:39:02,584
Tokaimura happened inside a nuclear facility, with experts and hospitals minutes away.

568
00:39:02,584 --> 00:39:07,588
Now imagine the same physics loose in the world, with no experts anywhere in sight.

569
00:39:08,733 --> 00:39:09,913
Chapter 13.

570
00:39:09,913 --> 00:39:11,737
The lost sources.

571
00:39:12,867 --> 00:39:18,633
Scattered across the world are thousands of small, sealed radioactive sources.

572
00:39:18,633 --> 00:39:21,887
In hospitals, factories, construction sites.

573
00:39:21,887 --> 00:39:25,066
Every year, some of them simply go missing.

574
00:39:26,200 --> 00:39:28,678
The safety community has a name for them.

575
00:39:28,678 --> 00:39:29,585
Orphan sources.

576
00:39:29,585 --> 00:39:35,447
And the orphans have a long record of finding their way to people who cannot know what they hold.

577
00:39:36,567 --> 00:39:45,526
The International Atomic Energy Agency has logged thousands of incidents of radioactive material lost, stolen, or found where it should never be.

578
00:39:45,526 --> 00:39:47,565
These are only the ones reported.

579
00:39:48,700 --> 00:39:50,944
Ciudad Juarez, Mexico, 1983.

580
00:39:50,944 --> 00:39:55,913
A hospital's disused cobalt-60 therapy unit is sold for scrap.

581
00:39:55,913 --> 00:40:02,806
The capsule breaks open in a junkyard, spilling thousands of tiny radioactive pellets.

582
00:40:03,933 --> 00:40:06,532
The pellets are melted down with the scrap.

583
00:40:06,532 --> 00:40:13,180
The contaminated steel becomes rebar and table legs, shipped to buildings across Mexico and the United States.

584
00:40:14,300 --> 00:40:21,443
It is only discovered when a delivery truck takes a wrong turn near the Los Alamos laboratory, and drives past a radiation sensor.

585
00:40:21,443 --> 00:40:23,861
The alarm goes off for a truckload of rebar.

586
00:40:25,000 --> 00:40:28,472
Hundreds of buildings are demolished or stripped.

587
00:40:28,472 --> 00:40:31,589
Several junkyard workers take serious doses.

588
00:40:31,589 --> 00:40:36,548
Estimates of eventual deaths from Juarez range from none to a handful.

589
00:40:36,548 --> 00:40:39,028
Once again, nobody can truly count.

590
00:40:40,167 --> 00:40:44,850
Sometimes the atom kills through the very machines built to heal.

591
00:40:44,850 --> 00:40:46,435
Zaragoza, Spain, 1990.

592
00:40:46,435 --> 00:40:51,478
A hospital's radiotherapy machine is repaired, and left miscalibrated.

593
00:40:52,600 --> 00:40:57,325
For 10 days, cancer patients receive massive overdoses with every treatment.

594
00:40:57,325 --> 00:41:01,926
At least 11 of them die from the radiation that was supposed to save them.

595
00:41:03,067 --> 00:41:04,482
Costa Rica, 1996.

596
00:41:04,482 --> 00:41:07,230
Same story, different arithmetic.

597
00:41:07,230 --> 00:41:14,806
A therapy source is replaced, the machine is miscalibrated, and 115 patients are overdosed.

598
00:41:14,806 --> 00:41:18,636
More than a dozen deaths are attributed to it.

599
00:41:19,767 --> 00:41:26,846
But the most intimate lost-source story of all comes from a Soviet apartment block, in the city of Kramatorsk, Ukraine.

600
00:41:27,967 --> 00:41:36,263
In around 1980, a tiny cesium-137 capsule, lost years earlier at a quarry, ends up mixed into gravel.

601
00:41:36,263 --> 00:41:39,631
The gravel becomes a concrete wall panel.

602
00:41:39,631 --> 00:41:42,178
The panel becomes apartment 85.

603
00:41:43,300 --> 00:41:49,814
The capsule sits inside the wall of a children's bedroom, silently dosing whoever sleeps against it.

604
00:41:49,814 --> 00:41:53,331
In 1981, a teenage girl living there dies of leukemia.

605
00:41:54,467 --> 00:41:57,015
Then her younger brother dies.

606
00:41:57,015 --> 00:41:58,544
Then their mother.

607
00:41:58,544 --> 00:42:00,413
A new family moves in.

608
00:42:00,413 --> 00:42:02,026
Their son dies too.

609
00:42:02,026 --> 00:42:05,509
Doctors blame heredity, then coincidence.

610
00:42:05,509 --> 00:42:07,633
The wall keeps radiating.

611
00:42:08,767 --> 00:42:14,232
It takes until 1989 for a determined father to demand a radiation survey.

612
00:42:14,232 --> 00:42:16,852
The meter screams against one wall.

613
00:42:16,852 --> 00:42:19,772
The capsule is cut out of the concrete.

614
00:42:19,772 --> 00:42:22,168
It had been killing for 9 years.

615
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:26,593
Every lost-source disaster is the same story.

616
00:42:26,593 --> 00:42:29,081
The atom does not announce itself.

617
00:42:29,081 --> 00:42:35,813
It waits, patiently, inside whatever we make of it, until someone finally thinks to measure.

618
00:42:36,933 --> 00:42:41,790
By the 21st century, humanity believed it had learned these lessons.

619
00:42:41,790 --> 00:42:47,931
Then, on an ordinary Friday afternoon in Japan, the ocean itself put them to the test.

620
00:42:49,067 --> 00:42:49,960
Chapter 14.

621
00:42:49,960 --> 00:42:52,071
The wave and the meltdown.

622
00:42:53,200 --> 00:42:54,582
March 11, 2011.

623
00:42:54,582 --> 00:42:56,610
2:46 in the afternoon.

624
00:42:56,610 --> 00:43:00,757
Off the coast of Japan, the seafloor lurches.

625
00:43:00,757 --> 00:43:06,287
A magnitude 9.0 earthquake, among the largest ever recorded.

626
00:43:07,433 --> 00:43:11,903
The quake triggers a tsunami up to 40 meters high in places.

627
00:43:11,903 --> 00:43:14,957
It erases whole towns from the coastline.

628
00:43:14,957 --> 00:43:18,979
Around 18,500 people are dead or missing by nightfall.

629
00:43:20,100 --> 00:43:24,099
On that coastline stands the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant.

630
00:43:24,099 --> 00:43:28,689
Its reactors survive the earthquake and shut down exactly as designed.

631
00:43:28,689 --> 00:43:30,131
Then the wave arrives.

632
00:43:31,267 --> 00:43:38,113
The tsunami overtops the seawall and drowns the emergency generators, which were installed in basements.

633
00:43:38,113 --> 00:43:39,429
The plant goes dark.

634
00:43:39,429 --> 00:43:41,141
No power means no cooling.

635
00:43:42,267 --> 00:43:45,100
Over the next days, 3 reactor cores melt down.

636
00:43:45,100 --> 00:43:54,153
Hydrogen explosions blow the tops off the reactor buildings on live television, while workers fight in the dark with car batteries and fire trucks.

637
00:43:55,300 --> 00:44:01,164
A skeleton crew stays behind as the site becomes a labyrinth of rubble and radiation.

638
00:44:01,164 --> 00:44:03,785
The press names them the Fukushima 50.

639
00:44:03,785 --> 00:44:06,062
None of them dies of their doses.

640
00:44:07,200 --> 00:44:11,666
More than 150,000 people are evacuated from the surrounding countryside.

641
00:44:11,666 --> 00:44:17,806
And here, the story becomes uncomfortable, because the numbers refuse to behave the way you expect.

642
00:44:18,933 --> 00:44:22,560
How many people did Fukushima's radiation kill?

643
00:44:22,560 --> 00:44:25,570
Directly, in the disaster itself, none.

644
00:44:25,570 --> 00:44:30,740
Not one worker, not one resident, died of acute radiation sickness.

645
00:44:31,867 --> 00:44:35,517
In 2018, Japan attributed 1 death to the radiation.

646
00:44:35,517 --> 00:44:42,316
A plant worker who had spent the crisis measuring doses, and died of lung cancer 7 years later.

647
00:44:43,433 --> 00:44:45,542
But the evacuation itself killed.

648
00:44:45,542 --> 00:44:54,744
Hospital patients and elderly residents moved in freezing buses, nursing home evacuees without care, years of displacement, stress, and suicide.

649
00:44:55,867 --> 00:45:06,473
Japanese authorities have certified over 2,000 disaster-related deaths in Fukushima prefecture, deaths from the evacuation and its aftermath rather than from radiation itself.

650
00:45:07,600 --> 00:45:10,328
That is Fukushima's unsettling lesson.

651
00:45:10,328 --> 00:45:12,482
The atom killed almost no one.

652
00:45:12,482 --> 00:45:17,866
Our terror of the atom, and the wave that started it all, killed thousands.

653
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:21,327
It is a strange entry on this list.

654
00:45:21,327 --> 00:45:24,252
And it forces the strangest question of all.

655
00:45:24,252 --> 00:45:28,639
What about the disasters that never happened, but very nearly did?

656
00:45:29,767 --> 00:45:30,594
Chapter 15.

657
00:45:30,594 --> 00:45:32,849
The ones that almost happened.

658
00:45:33,967 --> 00:45:42,356
Everything you have seen so far, every accident on this list, is small print compared to what the atom was actually built to do.

659
00:45:42,356 --> 00:45:45,043
And several times, it very nearly did it.

660
00:45:46,167 --> 00:45:47,923
Damascus, Arkansas.

661
00:45:47,923 --> 00:45:49,678
September 18, 1980.

662
00:45:49,678 --> 00:45:55,963
Inside a silo sleeps a Titan 2 missile carrying a 9 megaton warhead.

663
00:45:55,963 --> 00:45:58,366
About 600 times Hiroshima.

664
00:45:59,500 --> 00:46:03,791
During routine maintenance, a technician's socket wrench slips.

665
00:46:03,791 --> 00:46:09,444
The socket falls roughly 20 meters, bounces, and punctures the missile's fuel tank.

666
00:46:09,444 --> 00:46:12,509
The silo begins filling with explosive vapor.

667
00:46:13,633 --> 00:46:16,601
Just after 3 in the morning, the fuel detonates.

668
00:46:16,601 --> 00:46:22,227
The blast throws the silo's 700 ton door aside and hurls the warhead itself into the night.

669
00:46:23,367 --> 00:46:31,221
Airman David Livingston, 22 years old, part of the team sent in to vent the silo, dies of his injuries.

670
00:46:31,221 --> 00:46:34,271
The warhead is found in a ditch, intact.

671
00:46:34,271 --> 00:46:35,644
Its safeties held.

672
00:46:36,767 --> 00:46:39,166
Goldsboro, North Carolina, 1961.

673
00:46:39,166 --> 00:46:45,313
A B-52 breaks apart in midair, and its two hydrogen bombs fall on the countryside.

674
00:46:45,313 --> 00:46:49,436
On one of them, the arming sequence begins as it falls.

675
00:46:50,567 --> 00:46:55,937
By some accounts, 3 of the 4 stages toward detonation completed on the way down.

676
00:46:55,937 --> 00:47:02,113
What stood between North Carolina and a multi-megaton explosion was, in essence, one switch.

677
00:47:03,233 --> 00:47:06,740
A declassified assessment later put it in one sentence.

678
00:47:06,740 --> 00:47:13,499
One simple, dynamo-technology, low voltage switch stood between the United States and a major catastrophe.

679
00:47:14,633 --> 00:47:17,330
And then there is October 27, 1962.

680
00:47:17,330 --> 00:47:19,257
The Cuban missile crisis.

681
00:47:19,257 --> 00:47:28,504
Deep in the Caribbean, American ships are dropping practice depth charges to force a Soviet submarine, B-59, to surface.

682
00:47:29,633 --> 00:47:33,912
What the Americans do not know: B-59 carries a nuclear torpedo.

683
00:47:33,912 --> 00:47:36,765
The boat has been out of contact for days.

684
00:47:36,765 --> 00:47:42,877
The air is failing, the heat unbearable, and the crew believes war may already have begun.

685
00:47:44,000 --> 00:47:46,887
The captain wants to fire the nuclear torpedo.

686
00:47:46,887 --> 00:47:53,664
Accounts differ on the details, but launching reportedly required agreement from the senior officers aboard.

687
00:47:53,664 --> 00:47:54,919
One of them says no.

688
00:47:56,067 --> 00:47:58,045
His name is Vasili Arkhipov.

689
00:47:58,045 --> 00:48:00,306
He argues for surfacing instead.

690
00:48:00,306 --> 00:48:08,501
The submarine rises, the torpedo stays in its tube, and the world continues, unaware of how close the vote had been.

691
00:48:09,633 --> 00:48:13,175
There have been dozens of documented close calls.

692
00:48:13,175 --> 00:48:20,474
False alarms from training tapes, from moonrise, from a flock of geese, from a 46 cent computer chip.

693
00:48:21,600 --> 00:48:27,712
In 1983, a Soviet early-warning system reported 5 incoming American missiles.

694
00:48:27,712 --> 00:48:32,950
Duty officer Stanislav Petrov judged it a false alarm, and waited.

695
00:48:32,950 --> 00:48:33,982
He was right.

696
00:48:33,982 --> 00:48:36,046
It was sunlight on clouds.

697
00:48:37,167 --> 00:48:41,815
The atom's greatest kill count is the one that never happened.

698
00:48:41,815 --> 00:48:51,038
It exists only as a shadow, held back, so far, by luck, by safeties, and by a handful of individual human beings saying no.

699
00:48:52,167 --> 00:48:53,013
Chapter 16.

700
00:48:53,013 --> 00:48:55,014
What the photographs mean.

701
00:48:56,133 --> 00:48:59,172
Look back at the photographs one more time.

702
00:48:59,172 --> 00:49:00,443
The factory girls.

703
00:49:00,443 --> 00:49:01,503
The physicists.

704
00:49:01,503 --> 00:49:02,704
The firefighters.

705
00:49:02,704 --> 00:49:04,188
The scrapyard family.

706
00:49:04,188 --> 00:49:07,862
Almost no one in them looks like they are in danger.

707
00:49:09,000 --> 00:49:11,688
That is the signature of this killer.

708
00:49:11,688 --> 00:49:13,286
Fire announces itself.

709
00:49:13,286 --> 00:49:15,103
The sea announces itself.

710
00:49:15,103 --> 00:49:22,949
The atom kills invisibly, patiently, and often politely, letting its victims feel fine as they begin to die.

711
00:49:24,067 --> 00:49:27,778
It has killed through greed, at the dial factories.

712
00:49:27,778 --> 00:49:30,544
Through hurry, at Mayak and Tokaimura.

713
00:49:30,544 --> 00:49:32,582
Through secrecy, at Kyshtym.

714
00:49:32,582 --> 00:49:36,658
Through a slipped screwdriver, and a bucket, and a wall.

715
00:49:37,800 --> 00:49:43,980
Count the confirmed dead of every accident in this story, and the number lands in the hundreds.

716
00:49:43,980 --> 00:49:49,249
Add the disputed statistical deaths, and it may climb into the tens of thousands.

717
00:49:49,249 --> 00:49:51,201
Nobody will ever know exactly.

718
00:49:52,333 --> 00:49:55,101
And yet, here is the uncomfortable twist.

719
00:49:55,101 --> 00:50:06,439
Measured per unit of energy, nuclear power has killed far fewer people than the coal and oil we burned instead, whose air pollution kills millions, quietly, every year.

720
00:50:07,567 --> 00:50:13,142
The same physics that hollowed a dial painter's jaw lights entire cities.

721
00:50:13,142 --> 00:50:15,585
It sterilizes medical equipment.

722
00:50:15,585 --> 00:50:18,640
It treats the cancers it can also cause.

723
00:50:18,640 --> 00:50:20,015
It is not a demon.

724
00:50:20,015 --> 00:50:23,528
It is a force, and it keeps our terms exactly.

725
00:50:24,667 --> 00:50:29,084
Every death in this story happened where respect ran out.

726
00:50:29,084 --> 00:50:33,191
A rule skipped, a warning buried, a source forgotten.

727
00:50:33,191 --> 00:50:34,895
The atom never cheats.

728
00:50:34,895 --> 00:50:36,213
It only collects.

729
00:50:37,333 --> 00:50:42,168
The people in these photographs paid for the knowledge we now take for granted.

730
00:50:42,168 --> 00:50:47,860
Every dosimeter badge, every interlock, every lead apron is a rule written in someone's name.

731
00:50:49,000 --> 00:50:54,540
So the next time you see that trefoil symbol on a door, understand what it really says.

732
00:50:54,540 --> 00:50:57,150
People died to learn what is behind this.

733
00:50:57,150 --> 00:50:59,188
Do not make them teach it again.

734
00:51:00,333 --> 00:51:03,469
If this story stayed with you, subscribe to mindwired.

735
00:51:03,469 --> 00:51:07,882
It genuinely decides whether we can keep making documentaries like this one.

736
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:12,904
Because the atom is only one chapter in a larger book.

737
00:51:12,904 --> 00:51:14,857
The ocean has its own list.

738
00:51:14,857 --> 00:51:18,906
Crushed submarines, saturation divers, vanished vessels.

739
00:51:18,906 --> 00:51:22,087
Photographs with somebody missing from them.

740
00:51:23,233 --> 00:51:24,620
That story is next.

741
00:51:24,620 --> 00:51:31,994
Until then, remember the girls who glowed, the men on the roof, and the child with the shining hands.

742
00:51:31,994 --> 00:51:33,381
The light was real.

743
00:51:33,381 --> 00:51:34,622
So was the price.
